// Expected: c1c1
//
// ML-KEM decapsulation recovers the encapsulation randomness, not just the
// shared secret: FIPS 203's Decaps decrypts the ciphertext back to the
// encapsulated message and re-derives the coins from it, because the
// Fujisaki-Okamoto re-encryption check cannot run without them. So whoever
// holds the decapsulation key learns r as well as ss.
//
// Bob leaks dkb, so the attacker decapsulates ct. Both queries fail: ss by
// the shared-secret reveal, r by the randomness reveal. Before the second
// reveal existed this model was c1c0, and r was secret against an attacker
// that had already decapsulated it.
//
// kem_decap_randomness_sealed.vp is the counterweight: without dkb neither
// falls, so this is not a blanket disclosure of the randomness.

attacker[passive]

principal Bob[
	knows private dkb
	ekb = PUBKEY(dkb)
	leaks dkb
]

Bob -> Alice: [ekb]

principal Alice[
	knows public ctx
	generates r
	ss, ct = KEM_ENCAP(ekb, r)
	generates n_e
	e = AEAD_ENC(ss, n_e, ctx, ctx)
]

Alice -> Bob: ct, n_e, e

queries[
	confidentiality? ss
	confidentiality? r
]
